{
  "id": "DrugBank_DB00258 - MONDO_745",
  "verbalization": "Calcium Acetate is implicated in the pathogenesis of Cardiac Arrest through a series of indirect but interconnected mechanisms. The drug is associated with Hypercalcemia Disease, which is a known contributor to cardiac arrhythmias and myocardial dysfunction. Hypercalcemia, in turn, is linked to Calcium Glucoheptonate and Calcium Glycerophosphate, both of which are metabolites that can disrupt cardiac electrophysiology and lead to arrhythmias. These metabolites are also linked to Nephrocalcinosis, a condition that can exacerbate cardiac stress by increasing renal workload and electrolyte imbalances. The evidence suggests that Calcium Acetate may contribute to Cardiac Arrest via a chain of metabolic and physiological effects, particularly through the accumulation of calcium-based metabolites that impair cardiac function. The consistent scores across the three paths indicate a robust, albeit indirect, relationship, highlighting the role of calcium metabolism in cardiac arrhythmias.",
